From: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] local: drm/msm/dpu: name the ctl reset that actually fails

DIAGNOSTIC. Adds no behaviour, only two error paths that were silent.

0143 instruments the wrong end of this bug. It reports CTL_SW_RESET bit 0 still
set at kickoff, and on hardware that report is accurate but misattributed: the
bit is set *earlier*, by a reset this driver issued and nobody checked.

dpu_hw_ctl_reset_control() is the only code that ever writes CTL_SW_RESET=1. It
polls 2 ms and returns -EINVAL on timeout. Of its two callers, only
dpu_encoder_helper_hw_reset() looks at the return; dpu_encoder_helper_phys_cleanup()
discards it. So a reset that never completes leaves the bit set, prints nothing,
and the next kickoff trips over it and blames a frame that had nothing to do
with it. Every log we have starts at that second event.

Evidence for the misattribution, from two failures on 2026-09-18:

  cold boot   4.306 vblank timeout        <- pipeline already stalled
              4.485 ctl1 stuck in sw reset
  hib entry  30.228 freezing user space   <- display disabled here, silently
             30.479 ctl1 stuck in sw reset
             30.529 vblank timeout

In both, the reset that set the bit is unlogged and off the front of the trace.
0143 also proved the bit is not a stale handover -- its at-init check has never
fired across every boot since it was added -- and that writing 0 back does not
clear it, so this is a reset in progress that never finishes, not a latched bit.

Two hunks:

- dpu_hw_ctl_reset_control(): pr_err on the failing poll, with the register read
  back. Silent on success, so a healthy boot stays quiet.

- dpu_encoder_helper_phys_cleanup(): stop discarding the return, and read the
  INTF timing-engine status when it fails. This is the discriminator.
  dpu_encoder_phys_vid_disable() stops the timing engine and only then calls
  cleanup, so the reset can be issued into a pipeline that has stopped clocking.
  timing_en=0 on every failure means the ordering is the bug and the fix belongs
  in the disable path; timing_en=1 means the ctl genuinely wedges under a running
  engine and the fix belongs in the ctl. frame_count/line_count separate "engine
  off" from "engine on but not advancing".

Expected on a healthy boot: nothing. On a failing one, one new line before the
first "stuck in sw reset", naming the caller and the engine state.

Compile-checked against the 0143 tree, both objects, no warnings. Shelve once it
has answered.

--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_hw_ctl.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_hw_ctl.c
@@ -437,8 +437,19 @@
 
 	pr_debug("issuing hw ctl reset for ctl:%d\n", ctx->idx);
 	DPU_REG_WRITE(c, CTL_SW_RESET, 0x1);
-	if (dpu_hw_ctl_poll_reset_status(ctx, DPU_REG_RESET_TIMEOUT_US))
+	if (dpu_hw_ctl_poll_reset_status(ctx, DPU_REG_RESET_TIMEOUT_US)) {
+		/*
+		 * This is the only place that ever sets the bit, and the only
+		 * caller that checks the return is the hw_reset path. When the
+		 * poll fails here the bit stays set and the *next* kickoff is
+		 * what reports it, so the failure gets pinned on a frame that
+		 * had nothing to do with it. Name it where it happens.
+		 */
+		pr_err("ctl%d sw reset did not complete in %d us (SW_RESET=%#010x)\n",
+		       ctx->idx, DPU_REG_RESET_TIMEOUT_US,
+		       DPU_REG_READ(c, CTL_SW_RESET));
 		return -EINVAL;
+	}
 
 	return 0;
 }
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_encoder.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_encoder.c
@@ -2259,12 +2259,31 @@
 {
 	struct dpu_hw_ctl *ctl = phys_enc->hw_ctl;
 	struct dpu_hw_intf_cfg intf_cfg = { 0 };
-	int i;
+	struct dpu_hw_intf_status intf_status = { 0 };
+	int i, rc;
 	struct dpu_encoder_virt *dpu_enc;
 
 	dpu_enc = to_dpu_encoder_virt(phys_enc->parent);
 
-	ctl->ops.reset(ctl);
+	/*
+	 * dpu_encoder_phys_vid_disable() stops the timing engine before it gets
+	 * here, so this reset can land on a pipeline that has already stopped
+	 * clocking. Upstream discards the return entirely. Record the engine
+	 * state alongside the failure: a reset that only ever fails with
+	 * timing_en=0 is a sequencing bug, not a wedged ctl.
+	 */
+	rc = ctl->ops.reset(ctl);
+	if (rc) {
+		if (phys_enc->hw_intf && phys_enc->hw_intf->ops.get_status)
+			phys_enc->hw_intf->ops.get_status(phys_enc->hw_intf,
+							  &intf_status);
+		DPU_ERROR_ENC(dpu_enc,
+			      "ctl %d reset failed at cleanup: intf%d timing_en=%u frame_count=%u line_count=%u\n",
+			      ctl->idx,
+			      phys_enc->hw_intf ? phys_enc->hw_intf->idx - INTF_0 : -1,
+			      intf_status.is_en, intf_status.frame_count,
+			      intf_status.line_count);
+	}
 
 	dpu_encoder_helper_reset_mixers(phys_enc);
 
